Counter semantics after sliding-window dedupe

Receiver default path
receiver.cpp line 129 constructs dedupe::Window with default --dedupe-window 65536.
receiver.cpp lines 159-163 publish frames accepted by dedupe::Window.
receiver.cpp lines 187-201 print dedupe counters when dedupe is enabled.

Old compatibility path
receiver.cpp lines 149-156 are reached only with --dedupe-disable. They preserve the old seq_id > old_last_seq behavior and count old_duplicates when a frame is not newer than old_last_seq.

Current counter meanings
accepted: count of unique data frames admitted by the dedupe window. Changed meaning versus the old path because reordered frames can now be accepted if they are inside the window.
duplicates: count of already-seen sequence numbers still inside the dedupe window. Changed meaning versus old_duplicates; it is true duplicate suppression, not merely seq_id <= last_seq.
too_old: count of frames with seq_id lower than the current dedupe base. New counter.
lost_confirmed: count of missing sequence numbers retired when the window slides past them. New counter and not equivalent to injected network loss under saturation.
window_slides: count of forced window advances caused by seq >= base_seq + window_size. New counter.
max_reorder_depth: max observed seq - base_seq after any forced slide. New counter.
old_duplicates: compatibility counter for --dedupe-disable; in default mode it remains printed but does not drive default dedupe behavior.
received: UDP datagrams received before FEC decode/publish.
published: frames published into the output SHM after FEC decode and dedupe/compat filtering.
rejected: malformed datagrams or decoder publish failures.
fec_parity_received: parity datagrams received by FEC decoder.
fec_recovered: frames reconstructed by parity.
fec_unrecoverable_gens: generations retired with parity present and more than one missing data frame.

max_reorder_depth pinning
In dedupe_window.h lines 41-47, if seq >= base_seq + size_, slide_to(seq - size_ + 1) runs before depth is computed. That means for a far-ahead frame, depth becomes size_ - 1. With the default 65536 window this pins max_reorder_depth at 65535. Under WSL saturation this should be read as window-exceeded evidence, not as an exact reorder distance.

lost_confirmed with zero injected loss
lost_confirmed can be nonzero when --test-drop-pct is 0 because it counts missing sequence numbers retired by the receiver window, regardless of cause. In the WSL loopback diagnostics, missing frames can come from sender SHM lapping, UDP/socket loss under local saturation, receiver backlog, or FEC/recovery ordering effects. The current counters do not distinguish these causes.

Double-counting or dead counters
No current counter is mechanically double-counting the same observed event inside its own definition. The confusing one is old_duplicates: it is live only in --dedupe-disable mode and printed as 0 in default dedupe mode. It is not dead code because --dedupe-disable uses it, but it is a compatibility-path counter and should not be mixed with default duplicates.

Counters trustworthy under saturation
Trustworthy as local classifications: accepted, duplicates, too_old, window_slides, old_duplicates when --dedupe-disable, fec_parity_received, fec_recovered, fec_unrecoverable_gens, sender fec_closed_by_k, sender fec_closed_by_timeout, sender lapped.
Not trustworthy as network-loss measurements under saturation: lost_confirmed, max_reorder_depth, consumer drop_rate, and any absolute latency/drop number from WSL2 loopback.

Recommended additional counters before final EC2 claims
sender_ring_lapped_sequences: number of skipped sequence positions, not only lapped events.
receiver_seq_jump_raw: raw seq - base_seq before clamp/slide.
receiver_gap_retired_by_slide: current lost_confirmed name or a split of lost_confirmed by cause if possible.
receiver_socket_overflow: SO_RXQ_OVFL ancillary counter if available.
receiver_fec_late_recovered_too_old: recovered frames rejected by dedupe as too_old.
receiver_fec_generation_evictions: decoder slots overwritten before completion.
sender_wire_data_datagrams and sender_wire_parity_datagrams: distinguish data/parity from repeat fan-out packets.
